   "translation": "Before Chinese characters reached Japan, Japanese had no writing. After it met Chinese characters and Chinese texts, Japanese gradually formed two kinds of character reading. One kind borrowed the sound the character had in the Chinese of the time, adjusted to the Japanese sound system: it is called on'yomi. The other kind takes the meaning of the character and uses it to write down the native sound of a word Japanese already had: that is kun'yomi.",
   "note": ""
  },
  {
   "n": "1.3",
   "text": "两类读法可以并存于同一个汉字。以“山”字为例，音读作“サン”（san），训读则读作“やま”（yama）。前者源自汉语字音，后者则直接对应日语固有词（读音不承袭汉语）。有些音读与汉语发音相近，所以对汉语使用者较为熟悉，而训读则相对陌生。例如，“金”的训读有“かね”（kane），音读则有“キン”（kin）和“コン”（kon）。",
